WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.000
In my previous video, I talked about what quantum&nbsp;
computing is and what makes it special. But as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.400
a developer, I'm sure you want to know how to&nbsp;
actually write a piece of code that would run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.520
on a common computer. But before we dive into&nbsp;
that, let's just do a little bit of a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:25.680
recap. Instead of the classical bits of 0s and 1s,&nbsp;
quantum computers use qubits. A qubit can be a 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:35.200
a 1, or any linear combination of the two.&nbsp;
And that is what we called a superposition.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.120
We can also entangle multiple qubits. So their
states become strongly correlated.  In order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.480
to change the states of qubits, we apply&nbsp;
a series of gates, similar to the classical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.840
logic gates. And in the end, we want to measure&nbsp;
these qubits so we can get the results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:02.160
So how do we take all these concepts into&nbsp;
code? And the answer is simple. We use a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.960
quantum software development kit. In&nbsp;
this video, we will be using Qiskit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.400
Which is the most widely used quantum SDK today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.760
Qiskit is based on Python, which is fairly simple&nbsp;
to learn, even if you've never used it before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:25.840
So let's write a simple program in his Qiskit.&nbsp;In this program, we will use two qubits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.880
We will put one into superposition,&nbsp;entangle it with the other, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.840
do a measurement of both of them. And&nbsp;
of course, all that is done using gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:59.200
So let's start by importing quantum circuit from&nbsp;
Qiskit. We then can create a quantum circuit&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.840
with two quantum registers&nbsp;
and two classical registers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.160
The quantum registers are used for quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:23.840
computation. One for each qubit. And the classical&nbsp;
registers are used to store the measured results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.120
We need a classical registers because&nbsp;
even though the physical world is quantum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.800
the majority of the classical&nbsp;
world is still classical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.000
And the classical registers allow us to bring&nbsp;
quantum information back into the classical world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.320
So the next thing we want to do is&nbsp;
apply some gates. And in this program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.000
we're going to apply two gates. The&nbsp;
first one is the Hadamard gate&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:59.120
on qubit 0. The Hadamard gate puts&nbsp;
the qubit into a superposition between 0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.960
and 1. That means it now has an equal&nbsp;
chance of being measured a 0 or 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.200
The next gate we need is the control&nbsp;
not gate,  or "cx" for short.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:22.240
The control not gate is a conditional two qubits gate. It has a control qubit

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.120
and the target qubit.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.480
Without superposition, the control&nbsp;
not gate is fairly simple to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:43.680
That is, it is as if the state of the control qubit is&nbsp;
1, then you flip the state of the target qubit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.160
And that's why it's called control not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.520
And because the states of least two&nbsp;
qubits are now strongly correlated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.320
we now say they are entangled. So the&nbsp;
last thing we want to do is actually do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.840
measurements so we can get the outputs. And we&nbsp;
do this by calling the measure all function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:13.120
And there you have it. We just wrote a simple&nbsp;
quantum program using Qiskit. Now, if you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.320
this program and run it a bunch of times on an&nbsp;
ideal quantum computer, you'll find out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:26.320
there's a 50% chance of the outputs being 00 and&nbsp;
50% chance of it being 11. But you would never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:36.400
be a 01 or 10. The 50/50 of the first&nbsp;
qubit comes from the superposition. And while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.000
we didn't explicitly change the state of the&nbsp;
second qubit, it got changed anyway because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.480
it is entangled with the first qubit.&nbsp;
So it changes with the first qubit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.600
So in this program, we created a quantum&nbsp;
circuit which operates at the same level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:03.520
as classical assembly language and allows you&nbsp;
to efficiently manipulate the qubits directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.920
However, if you're not fond of&nbsp;
playing with low level circuits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:15.040
Qiskit also offers a number of higher&nbsp;
level algorithms. For example, Qiskit has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:22.000
a package focusing on machine learning that has&nbsp;
a number of pre-built classes. You can take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:30.640
quantum kernel class, use it to train and test&nbsp;
data. You can then take this trained quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.320
kernel and pass it into a classical algorithm&nbsp;
such as the support vector classification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:41.760
from scikit-learn. Then you can then&nbsp;
accelerate your classical application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.280
Thanks for watching. If you have any questions,&nbsp;
please leave them in the comments below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.040
Also, please remember to Like this&nbsp;
video and Subscribe to our channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:58.880
so we can continue to bring you&nbsp;
content that matters to you.

